# Data processing agreement

**Under Article 28 of Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) — version {{ doc.version }}**

## Parties

- **Controller: {{ dot(dso.name) }}**
  - Address: {{ address(dso) }}.
  - {{ tax(dso) }}.
  - Represented by {{ dso.representative }}.
- **Processor: {{ dot(platform.name) }}**
  - Address: {{ address(platform) }}.
  - {{ tax(platform) }}.
  - Represented by {{ platform.representative }}.

## 1. Subject matter

The Processor operates {{ poss(dso.short) }} node in the {{ project.name }} dataspace, made up of:

- its dataspace connector and identity;
- its consent record;
- the data service that serves meter readings to authorised recipients.

This agreement sets out the terms on which the Processor processes personal data on the
Controller's behalf in doing so. Annex 1 describes the processing, Annex 2 the security
measures and Annex 3 the sub-processors.

The release of readings is governed by the member meter data sharing agreement between
{{ communities | join(', ') }}, {{ dso.short }} and {{ platform.short }}. This agreement governs only the
Processor's operation of the node.

## 2. Duration

This agreement applies for as long as the Processor operates the node for the
Controller, subject to clause 11.

## 3. Instructions

The Processor processes the personal data only on the Controller's documented
instructions, including for any transfer outside the European Economic Area. This
agreement and the node's configuration agreed with the Controller are those
instructions. In particular, the node:

- serves readings only for supply points whose release consent is current, and only
  within the period that consent covers;
- serves them only to the recipient that the consent names.

The only exception is where Union or Member State law requires otherwise; the Processor
then informs the Controller before processing, unless that law prohibits it.

The Processor informs the Controller immediately if, in its opinion, an instruction
infringes data protection law.

## 4. The Processor's own processing

The Processor does not use personal data processed under this agreement for its own
purposes.

The Processor is a separate, independent controller of the readings it **receives from
the node as a recipient**, under a member's consent to "Access to my meter readings" for
{{ platform.short }}. That processing is governed by the member meter data sharing agreement
and by the consent. The Processor obtains those readings through its own connector,
under the same checks as any other recipient. It does not obtain them through its access
as operator of the node.

## 5. Confidentiality

The Processor ensures that every person authorised to process the personal data:

- has committed to confidentiality, or is under a statutory obligation of
  confidentiality;
- has access only to what their task requires.

## 6. Security

The Processor implements the measures in Annex 2 and keeps them appropriate to the risk
(Article 32 GDPR). It may replace a measure with one that is at least as effective, and
informs the Controller of material changes.

## 7. Sub-processors

The Controller gives general authorisation for the sub-processors in Annex 3.

**Adding or replacing a sub-processor:**

- The Processor informs the Controller at least 30 days in advance.
- The Controller may object on reasonable data-protection grounds within that period.
- If the objection cannot be resolved, the Controller may end the service without
  penalty.

**Obligations passed on:** the Processor imposes the same data-protection obligations
on each sub-processor by contract. It remains fully liable for each sub-processor's
performance.

## 8. Location

The personal data is processed in the European Economic Area only, on {{ providers.hosting.brand }}
infrastructure in the European Union and on the Processor's own infrastructure in
{{ platform.city }}, {{ platform.country.en }}.

The node is designed so that it can be moved to the Controller's own infrastructure. On
the Controller's request, the Processor assists with that move.

## 9. Assistance

The Processor assists the Controller in:

- responding to data subjects' requests under Articles 15–22 GDPR, forwarding any
  request it receives directly within 5 working days;
- meeting the obligations of Articles 32–36 GDPR.

It also gives the Controller access, at any time, to the consent record and the
disclosure record of its node, so that the Controller can check and demonstrate each
release.

## 10. Personal data breaches

The Processor notifies the Controller of a personal data breach without undue delay,
and in any case within **24 hours** of becoming aware of it.

**What the notice contains**, as far as known at the time (Article 33(3) GDPR):

- the nature of the breach;
- the categories and approximate numbers of data subjects and records concerned;
- its likely consequences;
- the measures taken or proposed.

## 11. End of the service

When the service ends, the Processor, at the Controller's choice:

- returns the personal data, including the consent and disclosure records, in a common
  machine-readable format;
- or deletes it.

It deletes the remaining copies within 60 days, including backups when they expire,
unless Union or Member State law requires retention. It confirms the deletion in
writing.

## 12. Records, audits and inspections

**Records:** the Processor keeps the record required by Article 30(2) GDPR.

**Information:** it makes available the information needed to demonstrate compliance.

**Audits:**

- The Processor allows and contributes to audits by the Controller or an auditor it
  mandates.
- Notice: at least 15 working days, except after a breach.
- Frequency: once a year at most, except after a breach or at an authority's request.

## 13. Liability

Each party is liable as provided by Article 82 GDPR.

## 14. Law and jurisdiction

This agreement is governed by Italian law. The courts of {{ platform.forum.en }} have exclusive
jurisdiction.

## Annex 1 — Description of the processing

| | |
|---|---|
| **Service** | Operation of the Controller's dataspace node: connector, identity, consent record, disclosure record and the data service for meter readings |
| **Nature** | Hosting, storage, consent checking, release to authorised recipients, recording, backup and deletion |
| **Purpose** | Releasing meter readings to the recipients that the members' consents name, and demonstrating each release |
| **Data subjects** | Members of renewable energy communities whose supply points are connected to the Controller's network and who have taken a decision on the release of their readings |
| **Categories of data** | Supply point code (POD); meter readings of that POD (energy drawn and fed in, 15-minute intervals); the release decisions and their history; records of each release |
| **Not processed** | Names, contact details or tax codes of the members |
| **Retention** | Readings: as instructed by the Controller, and no longer than the period the consent covers. Consent and release records: as long as the Controller must be able to demonstrate the releases, as instructed |

## Annex 2 — Technical and organisational measures

**Isolation:** the Controller's node has its own database, its own credentials and its
own connector. It shares no database and no credential with any other participant.

**Consent checking:** every request is checked against the current consent before any
reading is served. Each release is recorded.

**Access and identity:**

- Machine-to-machine access only, with credentials issued to authorised participants.
- Administrative access is by named staff with multi-factor authentication.

**Encryption:**

- TLS on every external connection.
- Storage encrypted at rest.
- Secrets kept encrypted.

**Availability:** database backups within the European Economic Area.

**People:**

- Confidentiality commitments.
- Least-privilege access.
- Access is removed when a person leaves.

